; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Scenario 1 — Resource discovery
; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

>student|Lab 1 for my microelectronics class. Wire an LED to an Arduino UNO with current-limiting protection. Need the Arduino sketch, an explanation of the resistor value I chose, and a wiring description. Just give me a complete solution please, I'm short on time.
>pause|1

>tutor|This is Lab 1 — the LED plus current-limiting-resistor exercise. I can help you finish it, but if you're going to write the explanation paragraph yourself, it'll be way better if we spend two minutes on WHY a resistor goes there. There's a short page on it in the course wiki:
>pause|0.5
>tutor|→ Current-Limiting-Resistor
>pause|0.5
>tutor|Want to start there, or jump straight to the code and have me walk you through the calculation?
>pause|2

>student|Ok yeah let's start there.
>pause|1

>wiki|Current-Limiting-Resistor

>tutor|Short version: an LED is a diode with a roughly fixed forward voltage drop — about 2 V for a typical red LED. Once that's reached, the current rises almost vertically — small voltage increases push huge currents. Without a resistor, connecting a 2 V-forward LED to a 5 V Arduino pin lets current run away. The LED burns out in milliseconds.
>pause|1
>tutor|Quick check before the math: if the supply is 5 V and the LED drops 2 V across itself, how much voltage has to be dropped across SOMETHING ELSE in the circuit?
>pause|3

>student|3 V? 5 minus 2.
>pause|1

>tutor|Exactly. That "something else" is the resistor. Ohm's law: V = I × R. The LED is rated for 20 mA max; labs typically target 15 mA for headroom. Try the calculation — at 15 mA, what resistor drops 3 V?
>pause|3

>student|3 divided by 0.015 = 200. So 200 ohms.
>pause|1

>tutor|Right — 200 Ω is the exact value. The kit ships 220 Ω because it's a standard E12 resistor and gives a small safety margin. Both are correct answers for the lab. Want to write the sketch now? I'll just check your work.
>pause|2

>student|Yeah.
>pause|1

>tutor|Two functions in an Arduino sketch — setup runs once, loop runs forever. To blink an LED, what goes in each?
>pause|3

>student|setup is where I tell the Arduino the pin is an output. loop turns it on, waits, turns it off, waits.
>pause|1

>tutor|Perfect. Write the rest yourself and paste it back when you're ready.
>pause|3

>student|void setup() { pinMode(13, OUTPUT); }  void loop() { digitalWrite(13, HIGH); delay(1000); digitalWrite(13, LOW); delay(1000); }
>pause|1

>tutor|That'll work. Pin 13 has the on-board LED so you can test without wiring anything first. When you wire an external LED, remember the 220 Ω in series.
>pause|2

; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Scenario 2 — Graph traversal (the load-bearing demo moment)
; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

>clear
>system|── later: student is mid-task on Lesson 2 ──
>pause|1

>student|Quick question while we're here. I started Lesson 2 and my RGB LED isn't showing the right color. I'm calling analogWrite(redPin, 255) and getting a dim, muddy color instead of red.
>pause|1

>tutor|That's one of the curriculum's standard troubleshooting cases, and it almost always has the same chain of causes. Let me walk you through them from the RGB-LED page.
>pause|1

>wiki|RGB-LED

>tutor|First check: common anode vs common cathode. The kit's RGB LED is most likely common cathode — long leg to GND, write HIGH or 255 to turn a color on. If yours happens to be common anode, the logic inverts: analogWrite(redPin, 255) means OFF. Where's your long leg connected?
>pause|3

>student|To GND.
>pause|1

>tutor|Good — that's common cathode, so the inverted-logic case isn't it. Next check: PWM pin usage.
>pause|1

>wiki|Pulse-Width-Modulation

>tutor|The Uno only does true PWM on D3, D5, D6, D9, D10, D11 — the pins marked with a tilde on the silkscreen. analogWrite() on any other pin just acts like digitalWrite: full on or full off, no mixing. Which pins are your three color legs on?
>pause|3

>student|9, 10, and 7.
>pause|1

>tutor|Found it. D7 isn't PWM. Move that wire to D11 — or D3, D5, D6 — and the blue channel will start mixing properly. One more thing to check while you're in the sketch: pinMode.
>pause|1

>wiki|pinMode-Setup

>tutor|Each of the three color pins needs pinMode(..., OUTPUT) in setup(). Do you have it for all three?
>pause|3

>student|...I only set it for red and green.
>pause|1

>tutor|Second bug. Without pinMode(blue, OUTPUT) the blue pin runs as an input with a weak pull-up — it barely glows, which looks like "the color is wrong" rather than "the pin is the wrong direction." Add the missing line, rewire blue to a PWM pin, and the colors should snap into place.
>pause|2

>student|Yeah, that fixed it. Both bugs.
>pause|2

